  "text": "Subscribe to skip adsAdvertisementHealthA Look at How Many Cancers Are Caused by Infections WorldwideNearly one in eight cancers are caused by a pathogenJCBy Jake Currie2:00 PM CDT on October 1, 2026Add Nautilus to GoogleWhen microbiologist Barry Marshall wanted to prove stomach ulcers were primarily caused by the bacterium Helicobacter pylori, he did something dramatic—he drank a beaker full of the bugs. In just a few days, he started showing the first symptoms of peptic ulcer disease. Further research showed the bacteria were capable of causing stomach cancer as well, and Marshall, along with his colleague Robin Warren, earned a Nobel Prize for their efforts.Powered By 10 ShareNextStayOf course, bacteria aren’t the only infectious agents capable of causing cancer, the genetic underpinnings of cancer research began with a chicken virus over a century ago. Since then, we’ve discovered several more cancers whose origins lie in infections, and even parasites that have the power to propel our cells into uncontrollable growth mode. Now, a new study published in the Lancet Oncology is offering a glimpse into what the rates of cancers caused by these pathogens looks like worldwide. Read more: “The Trouble with Cancer Screening”Subscribe to skip adsAdvertisement",
